st get better at hiding.' But Nora Ellis had changed — hadn't she? The question followed her through the farmers market, past the wildflowers, into her dreams."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Choice', 'text': "the city job offer left town with the gift wrap and the almosts. Nora Ellis felt nothing but pity — and then not even that, because her arms were full of present tense.\n\nShe found him at the shore, where it started. 'I choose,' she said, breathless, 'the thing that scares me. I choose you. I've always chosen you — I just took seventeen years to say it.'\n\nher grandmother Rose cried at the news and denied it. The whole of the lakeside village seemed to conspire in their happiness: the bookshop stayed open late, the bakery made a cake, the wildflowers bloomed on schedule."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Promise', 'text': "He proposed with a handwritten letter — fitting, her grandmother Rose said, since it started everything. Nora Ellis said yes before he finished asking, because seventeen years is long enough to wait for a sentence.\n\nLate that night, on the shore, they stood where the boy and girl had stood. 'We made it,' he said. 'We were always going to,' she said, and believed it, the heart knowing first.\n\nsecond chances are real — Nora Ellis wrote it in the new journal, the one for the next seventeen years. In the lakeside village, under the stars, with love: the whole story, and only just beginning."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue in Wildflowers', 'text': "her grandmother Rose still diagnoses everything with pie. Her grandmother Rose's quilt is worn thin with love. the city job offer is a story they tell laughing, a ghost with no haunting left in it.\n\nsecond chances are real. In the lakeside village, in the wildflowers, in the salt air, in every sunset: the whole story, still beginning, never ending.\n\nYears later, a handwritten letter sits on the mantel still, a little faded, entirely cherished. The house is full of noise: children, dogs, her grandmother Rose